WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
Cloud Code can spawn a separate agent instance to take on a piece of work on its own.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.439
This lesson covers creating and configuring those subagents.

3
00:00:09.439 --> 00:00:11.960
We'll look at what a subagent actually is,

4
00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.479
how you can spawn one,

5
00:00:13.479 --> 00:00:15.680
how context isolation works,

6
00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:17.959
how to scope its tools and permissions,

7
00:00:17.959 --> 00:00:21.120
and when spawning is the right architecture call.

8
00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.479
Let's jump in. A subagent isn't another chat window.

9
00:00:25.479 --> 00:00:27.920
It's a separate agent instance spawned by

10
00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.239
a parent through the agent tool inside

11
00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:34.799
the same Cloud Code session and handed one delegated subtask.

12
00:00:34.799 --> 00:00:39.799
It runs in its own isolated context window and reports its results back.

13
00:00:39.799 --> 00:00:43.200
Context isolation is the part people underestimate.

14
00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.419
Each subagent starts fresh with an empty context window,

15
00:00:47.419 --> 00:00:50.759
and nothing passes from the parent automatically.

16
00:00:50.759 --> 00:00:52.959
Every piece of information the subagent

17
00:00:52.959 --> 00:00:55.520
needs has to be included in the handoff.

18
00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.799
The orchestrator is the parent that decomposes the goal,

19
00:00:58.799 --> 00:01:02.639
delegates the work, and synthesizes what comes back.

20
00:01:02.639 --> 00:01:06.760
It doesn't share its context window with anything it spawns.

21
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:09.279
Subagent, context isolation,

22
00:01:09.279 --> 00:01:12.279
and orchestrator carry the rest of this lesson.

23
00:01:12.279 --> 00:01:15.040
Cloud Code subagents are different from

24
00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.559
multi-agent patterns you'd build directly with the API.

25
00:01:18.559 --> 00:01:20.120
In the API context,

26
00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:23.760
a multi-agent system is usually a set of API completions,

27
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.879
you're orchestrating yourself in application code.

28
00:01:26.879 --> 00:01:31.800
Cloud Code subagents are separate agent instances inside one session.

29
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:34.040
They're not API calls you're managing.

30
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:37.720
They're spawned via the agent tool within the same session,

31
00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.879
each running with its own isolated context window

32
00:01:40.879 --> 00:01:43.739
and its own configured tools and permissions.

33
00:01:43.739 --> 00:01:45.440
This distinction matters.

34
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.080
Subagents in Cloud Code aren't

35
00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:51.919
the same thing as agent orchestration using the API.

36
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:54.400
They operate at a different level and have

37
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.599
different characteristics around context,

38
00:01:56.599 --> 00:01:59.199
isolation, and tool access.

39
00:01:59.199 --> 00:02:02.279
Two paths exist for creating a subagent.

40
00:02:02.279 --> 00:02:04.919
The first is a markdown definition file.

41
00:02:04.919 --> 00:02:08.119
Custom subagents are defined as markdown files,

42
00:02:08.119 --> 00:02:11.320
either in a .cloudagents folder in your project

43
00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:14.479
or a .cloudagents folder in your home directory.

44
00:02:14.479 --> 00:02:18.119
You create one by asking Cloud to write the file for you,

45
00:02:18.119 --> 00:02:21.100
or by editing it directly to define the subagents,

46
00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.619
identity, tools, and scope.

47
00:02:23.619 --> 00:02:26.380
The agent tool is the programmatic path.

48
00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:29.179
When a parent agent is running an agentic workflow,

49
00:02:29.179 --> 00:02:31.660
it can invoke the agent tool to spawn

50
00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.899
a subagent dynamically as part of that workflow.

51
00:02:34.899 --> 00:02:37.559
This is how delegation happens in code.

52
00:02:37.559 --> 00:02:39.059
The markdown file gives you

53
00:02:39.059 --> 00:02:41.899
a named reusable subagent definition.

54
00:02:41.899 --> 00:02:44.100
The agent tool is how subagents get

55
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.380
spawned during execution of a larger workflow.

56
00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:51.259
Four things get configured on a per subagent basis.

57
00:02:51.259 --> 00:02:54.779
Tool access controls which tools the subagent can invoke.

58
00:02:54.779 --> 00:02:56.740
Bash, file reads,

59
00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:59.740
file writes, web access, or others.

60
00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:01.580
Isolation is the next lever.

61
00:03:01.580 --> 00:03:03.740
A work tree gives the subagent

62
00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.460
its own copy of the repository to work in,

63
00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.020
and permission rules scope how

64
00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:09.860
far it can reach on the file system.

65
00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:12.539
Permissions control what the subagent is

66
00:03:12.539 --> 00:03:15.339
allowed to do within its configured scope.

67
00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:19.059
Subagents can have narrower permissions than the parent.

68
00:03:19.059 --> 00:03:21.500
The task description is the explicit set of

69
00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.380
instructions defining what the subagent should do.

70
00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:27.460
All four of these are intentional design choices.

71
00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:29.259
You're not just spawning a session,

72
00:03:29.259 --> 00:03:33.779
you're defining a scoped permissioned agent with a specific job.

73
00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:35.940
Context isolation is one of

74
00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:39.179
the most important things to internalize about subagents.

75
00:03:39.179 --> 00:03:41.059
When a subagent is spawned,

76
00:03:41.059 --> 00:03:42.339
it starts fresh.

77
00:03:42.339 --> 00:03:44.259
There's no automatic inheritance.

78
00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.139
The parent's conversation history,

79
00:03:46.139 --> 00:03:47.619
already read files,

80
00:03:47.619 --> 00:03:50.059
and prior work don't carry over.

81
00:03:50.059 --> 00:03:53.419
If the subagent needs information from the parent's session,

82
00:03:53.419 --> 00:03:57.660
the parent has to explicitly include it in the handoff instructions.

83
00:03:57.660 --> 00:04:00.500
The subagent's results are returned to the parent,

84
00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:02.339
which then synthesizes them.

85
00:04:02.339 --> 00:04:04.820
Isolation is actually a feature here.

86
00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:07.740
It prevents the subagent from being influenced by

87
00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:10.860
context that isn't relevant to its task.

88
00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.020
It keeps the subagent focused,

89
00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:16.940
but it means the handoff has to be deliberate and complete.

90
00:04:16.940 --> 00:04:22.019
By default, a subagent inherits the parent's tools and permission context,

91
00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:25.899
but you can configure it with a narrower set than the parent holds.

92
00:04:25.899 --> 00:04:30.660
A subagent that's inspecting files can be configured as read-only,

93
00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:33.299
meaning it can look but not write.

94
00:04:33.299 --> 00:04:37.100
A subagent working on a specific service can be scoped to

95
00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:41.019
that directory only with no access to adjacent paths.

96
00:04:41.019 --> 00:04:43.339
Restricting tool access reduces

97
00:04:43.339 --> 00:04:47.019
the blast radius if the subagent does something unexpected.

98
00:04:47.019 --> 00:04:51.339
This is the principle of least privilege applied at the subagent level.

99
00:04:51.339 --> 00:04:55.779
Give the subagent exactly the access it needs for its task,

100
00:04:55.779 --> 00:04:57.459
and nothing beyond that.

101
00:04:57.459 --> 00:05:02.660
Restricting below the parent is the intentional opt-in step.

102
00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:05.420
Knowing when to spawn a subagent and when to

103
00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:08.019
stay in line is a practical design decision.

104
00:05:08.059 --> 00:05:12.940
Subagents make sense when tasks are truly independent and can run in parallel.

105
00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:16.859
When a task needs a different tool set than the parent is using,

106
00:05:16.859 --> 00:05:18.779
or when scope isolation prevents

107
00:05:18.779 --> 00:05:21.700
context contamination between separate work streams.

108
00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:26.540
Staying in line makes sense when the task needs full parent context to complete,

109
00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:31.339
when the work is sequential and depends on prior steps in the same session,

110
00:05:31.339 --> 00:05:35.940
or when the task is simple enough that delegation overhead exceeds the value.

111
00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:39.140
The overhead of spawning a subagent is real.

112
00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:44.579
Don't spawn one unless the isolation or parallelism actually buys you something.

113
00:05:44.579 --> 00:05:48.660
The handoff is a design artifact, not an afterthought.

114
00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:50.220
When you spawn a subagent,

115
00:05:50.220 --> 00:05:53.260
you're writing the only instructions it will ever have.

116
00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:55.140
There's no shared context.

117
00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:59.220
There's no ability to ask a follow-up question from the parent mid-task.

118
00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:02.820
The handoff needs to include a precise task description,

119
00:06:02.899 --> 00:06:04.940
defining what done looks like,

120
00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:06.739
the necessary context,

121
00:06:06.739 --> 00:06:09.380
only what the subagent actually needs,

122
00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:10.899
and the output format,

123
00:06:10.899 --> 00:06:15.140
exactly what structure you expect the subagent to return.

124
00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:17.779
If any of these three are underspecified,

125
00:06:17.779 --> 00:06:20.739
the results will be ambiguous or unusable.

126
00:06:20.739 --> 00:06:26.100
Designing the handoff is the most important part of deploying a subagent effectively.

127
00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:30.820
People hear isolated and they picture complete isolation.

128
00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:33.299
That's not quite what we're talking about here.

129
00:06:33.299 --> 00:06:34.739
Context is walled off,

130
00:06:34.739 --> 00:06:38.660
so the parent's conversation and open files don't carry over.

131
00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:44.500
But tools and permissions come down from the parent unless you narrow them yourself.

132
00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.739
Those are separate dials.

133
00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:48.500
Once you see them as separate,

134
00:06:48.500 --> 00:06:53.380
the choice of what to configure on each one stops feeling arbitrary.

135
00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:55.059
That brings this one to a close.

136
00:06:55.059 --> 00:06:56.660
I'll see you in the next video.

